From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Date: Fri, 28 Oct 1994 12:25:31 -0400
Subject: [#94-10-578] Brain backup proposal

>From: minsky@media.mit.edu (Marvin Minsky)
>X-Message-Number: #94-10-509
>
>Consider that the more massive the effect,
>the less information they carry.
>

A good heuristic...

>>From: Jason Hamilton
>>
>>A neuron fires based on stimulus from one end, but once it fires, it
>>releases chemicals at the other end.  Whether those neurotransmitters
>>reach the next neuron (or whether they reach the "right" nerurons) is
>>largely due to a chemical balance that isn't recorded by your
>>hypothetical nano's.
>
>To first order, the largest effect by far is on the single neural cell
>surface on the other side of the synapse.  (No synapses connect to more
>than one other cell.)

Physically, yes.  However, certain substances such as NO are thought
to diffuse for short distances and have modulatory effects upon several
nearby cells - a functionally distributed synapse.  This may be a very
fundamental computational mechanism, but it's too early to tell.

>This is where the huge predominance of the
>information lies, and this -- the transsynaptic coeeficients is what the
>nanos should record most of.

Yes, this is what I meant about measuring the transfer functions - not
just coefficients though.  There is a lot of "context" in synaptic
transmission, some of it related to past firing history and some of
it to the activity in nearby cells.  An example of temporal effects
is habituation - a decrease over time in the response.  An example of
neighbor effects is modulation by second messengers: cell A may excite
cell B through a primary messenger like glutamate, but the transfer
function A->B may be magnified by the firing of a cell C that releases
serotonin and connects either to the end of cell A or to the dendrite
of cell B.

So to store the functionality of a synapse one would need to record
the transfer function in all the various contexts.  Whatever the
combinatorial size of the contexts, the nanos will have to record from
more than one neuron at a time.  They will also certainly not be able
to discard the connectivity information as suggested by Keith Lynch.
Also, for each neuron, we need to record the firing function given a
particular pattern of synaptic potentials at the dendrites - to capture
cell geometry and conductance effects.

>In any case, there's no evidence for the "chemical balance" that you
>postulate.  Or rather, because the entire brain is made of chemicals, there
>no reason to suppose that most of that information can't be inferred from
>the synaptic geometries.

Too much dopamine in certain parts of the brain can cause schizophrenia;
too little dopamine in others causes Parkinsonism (slowing of movement &
paralysis).  However, this is probably due to an overabundance or
underabundance of receptors for dopamine in various regions.  So you don't
need to record dopamine levels, but you should keep some info on
receptor densities.

Paul


