From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Fri, 28 Oct 1994 10:34:00 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-581] SCI: Many-Worlds FAQ

Michael Clive Price writes:
>What about:
>If we have:
>
>       |psi> = |1>*0 + |2>*1 + |3>*3   
>             = |2> + |3> + |3> + |3>
>
>Would you still count world1?  

No. I accepted the assumption in the standard proofs that zero
magnitude worlds don't "exist".
 
>The weights can't ignored and so we can
>only count worlds on a comparative basis when their weights are equal.

I'm having trouble parsing this. 

To review, I'm taking the standard "decoherence" approach to
many-worlds, where the total state vector can be broken up into a
basis according to which parts of the total state vector "cohere" or
"decohere" with each other.  I call each element of this
basis a "world", excluding zero vectors, and assume that statistical
mechanics assures this basis is finite.  Given these assumptions, I
indicated that while for an infinitely old universe many-worlds
implies ordinary quantum statistics, for a finite universe it implies
equi-probable statistics.  I suggested how a small non-linearity could
imply quantum statistics in the finite case as well.

