Subject: [#94-10-582] Slaves (was: controlling ai)

FH> *Nothing* is scarier to a slave-owning society than an
    autonomous, self-modifying slave.  Slave-owning societies are
    famously hostile to technological change (at the time of the
    CW the South had exactly *one* railroad line) for just this
    reason: any change at all might compromise the severity and
    relentlessness of their control.

RH> Er, this doesn't fit at all with the papers on the economics
    of U.S. slavery I've been reading for my graduate Economic
    History course in the last few weeks.  Slavery may be bad,
    but clever slaves were a prized asset, and slave owners
    weren't afraid of technology.

FH> If you are learning in your graduate economic course that the
    South was within an order of magnitude of the North in adopting
    the technologies of the first half of the nineteenth century --
    canals, hydropower, textiles, railroads, Eli Whitney's prefab
    assembly techniques (as in the manufacture of watches), the
    telegraph -- you should look into an immediate transfer.

A lower rate of technological adoption does not imply *fear* of
technology.  Many factors influence rates of adoption - I don't think
fear is that high on the list.  All I've read is consistent with slave
owners being individual profit-maximizers - they'd use a technology if
they thought they could make a profit.

The cotton gin was certainly adopted big time.  The chain gang was a
very important innovation that greatly increased slave productivity.
And compared to most societies with slaves in history, the US south
became efficient enough at breeding slaves that new-born slaves had a
positive market value.  So they bred slaves instead of importing them.

>And if you are being taught that the cultural regime of slavery
>was not entirely about control you should sue.  Just topick one
>example out of zillions, most -- maybe all -- of the Southern
>jurisdictions had laws against teaching slaves to read.  That
>sound to you like a regime that prized clever slaves?

Slave owners of course had to maintain control of slaves, but this
really wasn't that hard, and certainly didn't prevent making clever
slaves when such slaves could be more productive.  Many slaves were
educated, to serve as tutors, and slaves trained in skilled
"professions" had higher market value.  Slaves were just as good as
these jobs as non-slaves.  The reason they weren't used so much that
way is that slaves were *so* damn productive in chain gangs.

Here's an interesting theory, due to Domar, on why local folks were
often enslaved, into states like serfdom.  When there were lots of
folks and not so much land, wages drop to near subsistence, and land
prices are high.  Here a rich landed class can be supported, and there
isn't much point to slavery.  But when lots of folks got killed off,
and/or lots of new land became available, then wages go up and the
price of land plummets.  Then the only way to have a rich class is for
them to own people, instead of land.  So to stay rich, the old
landed-rich get the political powers to enslave the populace.

Robin Hanson

