Subject: [#94-10-584] Statism vs. "State" (was: Reading List?)

James Kobielus wrote 10/28/94: <I know it's not fun to have your basic
assumptions challenged.>

Actually, it *is* fun to have metaphysical assumptions challenged if it's done
intelligently.  I think what you mean is that it's not fun to have your dogma
challenged.

<you'll have to welcome--and respond clearly and nondefensively--to new posters>

I recall some mention of courtesy some time ago.  BTW, I think you meant
"nondefensively to--new posters," not "nondefensively--to new posters."

<If you remain an insular, condescending, know-it-all clique, your leverage will
never grow.>

This is patently false.  Look at the leftist liberal take-over of the
government, the media, the entertainment industry and the educational system.
The take-over was effected mostly over the last 30 years while remaining an
"insular, condescending, know-it-all clique" the whole time.

<Government agencies can be a great instrument for achieving leverage.  They're
no more inherently sinister and corrupt than private corporations...>

There is a fundamental error here.  Modern governments maintain the power of
life and death over their subjects.  People today are "for" the government, the
government is not "for" the people.  Don't forget this tyrannous quote from
1960:  "Ask not what your [government] can do for you, ask what you can do for
your [government]."  Corporations, or any other institution, religious or
secular, simply do not have the iron fist backing up their whims like the
government has, exercised under the guise of arbitrarily and capriciously
enforced laws and regulations.  No CEO can send in Janet Reno to save children
from abuse by torching them into cinders, and get away with it.

<Government has to be responsible--or at least has to respond--to most of the 
people most of the time.>

This is the horror of "Statism."  Most people are average, many with desires and
beliefs that drift hither and thither daily.  They are largely empty of
long-range purpose, meaning and discipline.  They can neither command nor obey
since they have been told that they are "equal" to their superiors.  They
recognize no one as an authority, no one worthy of respect, no one who can fill
their emptiness with something worthwhile to do.  They are locked into either
dogmatic or nihilistic worldviews.  Their standing order for the ship of state
is:  "Steady as she drifts."  They are incapable of leading a society forward.
Our foreign policy is conducted by whimsical public opinion responding to
whatever TV images were broadcast the night before.  This is a hallmark of
emptiness at the core of any society.  Any group pursuing a coherent vision of
progress, complete with standards of excellence, gets squashed through
government action responding to the envy and resentment contained in massive
public opinion against anyone so presumptuous as to be superior in abilities or
vision to the average.

Our form of government has devolved towards direct democracy, legally unequal
factions through the mechanism of group rights, and capricious rule by
individuals instead of law through the mechanism of activist judges and juries,
and unaccountable bureaucrats, regulating and enforcing by personal agendas.
These have historically always led to decay and violence.

A "State" is possible without "Statism."  It just doesn't look like today's
modern state.  A state is, in essence, a group of people with coherent
consensual purposes, a set of behavioral standards for civilization, and some
judicial framework (preferably polycentric).  It can easily exist in cyberspace,
it does not need common geography, common language, or common ethnicity.  It
does not need standards or law imposed hierarchically, these can arise
spontaneously.  It does need leadership because it can't achieve every possible
thing it wants simultaneously.  "State" has a bad name now, but by whatever
name, the concept is the same.

